course: dictionary
lesson: 200
title: Alas: a word of sorrow
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Alas is a small word with a big feeling. It is an interjection, a word that shows how the speaker feels, and it often comes at the start of a sentence. Alas shows sadness, sorrow or regret. «Alas! What am I saying?» The speaker has just said something foolish and is sorry about it, so the word shows regret.
explain: Look at the words around alas to work out the feeling. It is usually followed by a comma or an exclamation mark, and the words after it tell you what went wrong. In old stories it often comes just before something sad, such as «Alas, the prince was gone.»
explain: Today alas is old-fashioned, so you will mostly find it in old stories, poems and fairy tales. Some people also use it as a joke about small disappointments, like a cake that has all gone. Ask yourself: is the thing really sad, or is the writer teasing?
fact: Alas is an interjection: a word that shows feeling.
fact: «Alas» shows sadness, sorrow or regret.
fact: Alas is old-fashioned or literary, so it is common in old stories and poems.
fact: Today people sometimes use alas as a joke for small disappointments, such as «Alas, the cake was all eaten.»
fact: Alas is often followed by a comma or an exclamation mark.
exercise: «Alas! What am I saying?» What feeling does alas show here? | *Sorrow or regret | A question about the time | A loud noise | Great joy and a party
exercise: In an old fairy tale: «Alas, the prince was gone.» What feeling does alas show? | Excitement | Anger about the weather | *Sadness | Surprise at a birthday
exercise: A child says, «Alas, the cake was all eaten!» How is alas used here? | A happy shout of thanks | A question about baking | A deep, terrible tragedy | *A joking way of showing a small disappointment
test: In «Alas, it was too late», which word shows the feeling? | late | it | too | *Alas
test: What is the best meaning of alas in an old poem? | What a lovely morning | *Oh no, how sad | Please come here | Hurrah, we won
exam: Why might a modern writer use alas about a missed bus? | *To show a serious war has started | To say thank you for the ride | To ask the time politely | To make a small disappointment sound dramatic and funny
